  "helpFaq1Question": "What is On Chain Notes?",
  "helpFaq1Answer": "On Chain Notes lets you store notes permanently on a blockchain so content stays tamper-evident and traceable. Once published on-chain, it typically cannot be changed or removed.",
  "helpFaq2Question": "How do I create a note?",
  "helpFaq2Answer": "You can:\n1. Tap “Create note” on the home screen\n2. Or tap the “+” button on the notes page\n3. Add title, content, and images",
  "helpFaq3Question": "What save options are available?",
  "helpFaq3Answer": "There are three options:\n1. Draft: saved locally, editable anytime\n2. Local save: finalized locally\n3. On-chain: permanent on blockchain, not editable\n\nWe recommend using a draft first, then publishing when ready.",
  "helpFaq4Question": "What is on-chain save? Do I pay?",
  "helpFaq4Answer": "On-chain save stores your note on-chain permanently. You pay network fees (Gas) charged by the blockchain, not an app fee. Fees vary by network; many L2s are cheaper.",
  "helpFaq5Question": "Which networks are supported?",
  "helpFaq5Answer": "The app supports eight mainstream EVM networks, including Ethereum, BSC, Polygon, Arbitrum, Optimism, Avalanche, Fantom, and Base. You can switch networks from the home screen.",
  "helpFaq6Question": "How do I verify a note is on-chain?",
  "helpFaq6Answer": "After publishing, you get a transaction hash. In note details you can view the hash, use Verify, or copy the hash to a block explorer for that network.",
  "helpFaq7Question": "What is a recovery phrase? Why back it up?",
  "helpFaq7Answer": "A recovery phrase (often 12 English words) is the master key to recover your note account.\n\nIf you lose it, you may lose access forever.\nIf it leaks, your notes may be permanently lost or managed by others.\nThe app cannot recover it for you.\nWrite it on paper and store it safely.",
  "helpFaq8Question": "How do I back up my recovery phrase?",
  "helpFaq8Answer": "1. Open Profile\n2. Tap backup recovery phrase\n3. Read the warnings\n4. Write down all words in order\n5. Verify in order\n6. Finish backup\n\nDo this in a private environment.",
  "helpFaq9Question": "Recovery phrase vs security key?",
  "helpFaq9Answer": "Both can restore an note account.\n\nRecovery phrase: 12 words, easier to write down, derives keys (recommended backup).\n\nSecurity key: long hex string, harder to handle, single address (advanced users).",
  "helpFaq10Question": "How do I switch note accounts?",
  "helpFaq10Answer": "1. Tap the note account address on the home header\n2. Pick another note account in the selector\n3. Or go to Note account management to create/import\n\nYou can manage multiple note accounts.",
  "helpFaq11Question": "How do I see addresses on other chains?",
  "helpFaq11Answer": "In My note account:\n1. Expand the card with the arrow\n2. See supported chains and addresses\n3. Tap to copy\n\nFor EVM-compatible chains, the address format is the same.",
  "helpFaq12Question": "How do I customize an RPC node?",
  "helpFaq12Answer": "1. Profile → Network settings\n2. Choose a network\n3. Add node\n4. Enter name and RPC URL\n5. Test connection\n6. Set as default\n\nCustom RPC can improve speed and reliability.",
  "helpFaq13Question": "What rich text formats are supported?",
  "helpFaq13Answer": "Bold, italic, underline, headings (H1–H3), ordered/bullet lists, quotes, code blocks, and image upload—via the editor toolbar.",
  "helpFaq14Question": "Can on-chain notes be edited or deleted?",
  "helpFaq14Answer": "Generally not—that is a property of blockchains.\n\nPublished notes are permanent and tamper-evident.\nUse drafts to iterate, then publish only when sure. Avoid putting highly sensitive data on-chain.",
  "helpFaq15Question": "Why does on-chain publishing take time?",
  "helpFaq15Answer": "After fees are accepted, the transaction must be mined. Time depends on congestion and gas speed (fast often ~15–30s, standard ~1–2m, slow longer). Usually under a couple of minutes.",
  "helpFaq16Question": "What if I forgot my password?",
  "helpFaq16Answer": "You can re-import the note account with your recovery phrase and set a new password. Local data may be retained depending on your setup.\n\nRemember: password encrypts locally; recovery phrase is the ultimate recovery key—keep it backed up.",
